    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Diving Into Lists"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "NHC5oJ2jZ0kj"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Imagine having to take an inventory of all flora in a forest without a list at your disposal — seems near impossible, right? That's precisely the purpose Lists serve in Python. They let us organize data so that each item holds a definite position or an index. The index allows us to access or modify each item individually.\n",
        "\n",
        "Working with Lists is as simple as this:"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_eY4aiWnaAM-"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# Creating a list\n",
        "fruits = ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']\n",
        "print(fruits)\n",
        "print('*' * 50)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add a new element at the end\n",
        "fruits.append('date')\n",
        "print(fruits)\n",
        "print('*' * 50)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Inserting an element at a specific position\n",
        "fruits.insert(1, 'blueberry')\n",
        "print(fruits)\n",
        "print('*' * 50)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Removing a particular element\n",
        "fruits.remove('banana')\n",
        "print(fruits)\n",
        "print('*' * 50)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Accessing elements using indexing\n",
        "first_fruit = fruits[0]\n",
        "print(f'First fruit: {first_fruit}')\n",
        "last_fruit = fruits[-1]\n",
        "print(f'Last fruit: {last_fruit}')"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "xk-4RFjsaChK",
        "outputId": "9466536d-df68-4452-ede3-504e85e23203"
      },
